    insiders: ['Federal Reserve Bank of New York (senior lender and portfolio oversight institution)', 'Federal Reserve Board (authorizing authority)', 'Investment Support Office (New York Fed unit coordinating oversight and service providers)', 'BlackRock Financial Management (investment manager identified among advisors reviewing Maiden Lane asset eligibility)', 'Ernst & Young (advisor identified in the asset-eligibility review)', 'Bear Stearns (source of assets purchased by Maiden Lane LLC)', 'JPMorgan Chase (acquirer of Bear Stearns and subordinated lender to Maiden Lane LLC)', 'AIG and its insurance subsidiaries (parties to the Maiden Lane II and III transactions)', 'Auditors and other service providers (participants in the oversight framework)', 'US public (beneficiary of reported New York Fed net gains)'],
    quid_pro_quo: 'Senior public loans and subordinated private funding supported asset purchases. The New York Fed’s Investment Support Office worked with investment managers, auditors and other providers to oversee portfolios and prioritize repayment without disrupting markets.',
    theft_mechanism: 'Outsourced investment expertise operated within a stated public oversight framework. The source identifies BlackRock’s advisory role but does not establish a secret transfer of public authority, access to every Wall Street balance sheet or misuse of information for acquisitions.',
    who_gained: 'The overview reports full senior-loan repayment and net public gains of approximately $2.5 billion for Maiden Lane, $2.8 billion for Maiden Lane II and $6.6 billion for Maiden Lane III in its sale milestones. Vendor compensation and private parties’ net gains are not quantified here.',
    who_lost: 'The New York Fed bore lending risk during the facilities’ operation. Reported positive recoveries do not prove the intervention had no opportunity costs or distributional effects, but they contradict treating all funding as an unrecovered taxpayer loss.',
    legacy: 'Shows why crisis-portfolio oversight, vendor selection, information conflicts and financial outcomes are separate questions. Oversight described by an institution is not an independent assessment of its effectiveness.',
